\section{Example: Range}

\begin{note}
The built-in \lstinline{range} function does not actually return a list,
but an instance of the \lstinline{range} class.
See:
\begin{lstlisting}
>>> type(range(10))
<class 'range'>
>>> range(10)
range(0, 10)
\end{lstlisting}
In this section we see how they implement some of the container-like features of this class.
\begin{lstlisting}
>>> len(range(10))    # implemented by __len__ method
10
>>> 5 in range(10)    # implemented by __contains__ method
True
>>> range(10)[0]      # implemented by __getitem__ method
0
\end{lstlisting}
\end{note}
\end{minipage}
